WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.460
You've now learned so much about deep learning and sequence models that we can actually

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:11.820
describe a trigger word system quite simply, just on one slide as you see in this video.

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.140
But with the rise of speech recognition, there have been more and more devices you

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:20.340
can wake up with your voice and those are sometimes called trigger word detection systems.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:24.180
So let's see how you can build a trigger word system.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.700
Examples of trigger word systems include Amazon Echo, which is broken up with the

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:35.340
word Alexa, the Baidu dual OS power devices, broken up with the phrase Xiao Dooning Hao,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:40.500
Apple Siri, broken up with Hey Siri and Google Home, broken up with OK Google.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.100
So it stands to trigger word detection that if you have, say, an Amazon Echo in your living

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:49.980
room, you can walk in your living room and just say, Alexa, what time is it?

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:56.220
And have it wake up or be triggered by the word Alexa and answer your voice query.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:01.300
So if you can build a trigger word detection system, maybe you can make your computer do

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.180
something by telling it computer activate.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:11.420
One of my friends also works on turning on and off a particular lamp using a trigger word,

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.660
kind of as a fun project.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:18.380
But what I want to show you is how you can build a trigger word detection system.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.740
The literature on trigger word detection algorithm is still evolving.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.900
So there isn't wide consensus yet on what's the best algorithm for trigger word detection.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:31.060
So I'm just going to show you one example of an algorithm you can use.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.700
Now you've seen RNNs like this.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:38.540
And what we really do is take an audio clip, maybe compute spectrogram features and that

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:45.420
generates features, X1, X2, X3, audio features, X1, X2, X3, that you pass through an RNN.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:51.620
And so all that remains to be done is to define the target labels Y.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:59.380
So if this point in the audio clip is when someone just finished saying the trigger word,

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:04.900
such as Alexa or Sayadunihao or Hey Siri or Ok Google, then in the training sets, you

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:09.980
can set the target labels to be zero for everything before that point.

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.620
And right after that, to set the target label of one.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:19.900
And then if a little bit later on, you know, the trigger word was set again and the trigger

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:26.700
word was set at this point, then you can again set the target label to be one right

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.020
after that.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:34.500
Now this type of labeling scheme for an RNN, you know, could work.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.440
Actually, this will actually work reasonably well.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.820
One slight disadvantage of this is it creates a very imbalanced training set.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.420
So a lot more zeros than ones.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.740
So one other thing you could do that's just getting a little bit of a hack, but could

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:55.460
make the model a little bit easier to train is instead of setting only a single time

00:02:55.460 --> 00:03:00.940
set to output one, you can actually make an output a few ones for several times or for

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:04.900
a fixed period of time before reverting back to zero.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:15.180
So and that slightly evens out the ratio of ones to zeros.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.140
But this is a little bit of a hack.

00:03:17.140 --> 00:03:24.740
But if this is when in the audio clip, a trigger word is set, then right after that,

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.420
you can set the target label to one.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:31.140
And if this is the trigger word set again, then right after that is when you want the

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:34.500
RNN to output one.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.100
So you get to play more of this as well in the programming exercise.

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.020
But I think you should feel quite proud of yourself.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:47.980
You've learned enough about deep learning that it just takes one picture and one

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.420
slide to describe something as complicated as trigger word detection.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:57.860
And based on this, I hope you'll be able to implement something that works and

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.740
allows you to detect trigger words.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.260
But you see more of this in the programming exercise.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:09.500
In this course on sequence models, you learned about RNNs, including both GRUs

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:10.980
and LSTMs.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.420
And then in the second week, you learned a lot about word embeddings and how to

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.580
learn representations of words.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:23.300
And then in this week, you learned about the attention model as well as how to

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:26.180
use it to process audio data.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:30.180
And I hope you have fun implementing all of these ideas in this week's

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.420
programming exercise.
